        <div id="finish-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Finish Types: What Sealer Means Outdoors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Exterior wood finishes sit on a spectrum from pure water repellent to opaque coating. At one end are clear repellents that make water bead but offer almost no sun protection. Next come penetrating oils, clear or lightly toned, which soak in and harden inside the wood. Then come semi-transparent stains with real pigment, solid stains that hide grain, and finally paint. The more pigment a finish carries, the longer it resists UV.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating finishes are the safest choice for most outdoor wood because they cannot peel. When they wear, the wood looks drier and lighter, and a cleaning and fresh coat restores it. Film finishes, from spar varnish to solid stain and paint, look richer at first but must be kept intact; once water gets behind a film, it lifts and the old coat has to be scraped or stripped.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil versus water base is the other big choice. Oil-based penetrating finishes go deeper in dense and weathered wood, warm the color and fade gradually. Water-based finishes dry faster, have less odor, clean up with soap and water and are legal in states with strict limits on volatile organic compounds. Some newer water-based products use transparent oxides to add UV defense without much visible color.
          </p>

          <p>
            Species changes the answer. Pine, fir and pressure-treated lumber accept almost any finish but gain the most from pigment. Cedar and redwood absorb oil readily and look best with a toned penetrating finish. Dense hardwoods such as teak, ipe and mahogany resist absorption, so they need thin oils formulated for hardwood and careful wiping. If you are working on a deck specifically, our <a href="/best-deck-sealant/">deck sealant guide</a> covers the products that hold up to foot traffic.
          </p>

