        <div id="prep-by-surface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Prep by Surface: What Each Job Needs First
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Weathered gray siding and fences.</strong> The gray layer is loose, sun-damaged fiber, and finish applied over it fails along with it. An oxygen-based wood cleaner, a soft scrub and a low-pressure rinse remove most of it. Follow with an oxalic acid brightener to bring back the wood's color and neutralize the cleaner. Two dry days later, the wood is ready for a penetrating finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>New smooth lumber.</strong> Planed boards can carry mill glaze, a hard, slick surface that blocks absorption. A simple test is to drip water on the board; if it beads on bare new wood, the surface is closed. A light sanding with 60 to 80 grit, or a few months of weathering, opens it up. Pressure-treated lumber also has to dry out from treatment before it will take any finish at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Previously finished wood.</strong> Identify what is on it before you buy anything. If water soaks in and there is no film, a penetrating finish can go straight on after cleaning. If there is a film that is peeling, scrape and strip it. If there is a sound solid stain or paint, stay with a compatible solid product, because a penetrating sealer has nothing to soak into.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Outdoor furniture.</strong> Wash with a mild detergent or a teak cleaner, rinse and let the piece dry fully indoors or under cover. Sand lightly with a fine grit to knock down raised fibers, wipe away dust and seal. Small pieces reward the extra care, and the whole job takes an afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Logs and rough timbers.</strong> Log-home finishes usually call for media blasting or a log-specific cleaner to remove old coatings and gray wood, and for chinking and check sealing to be finished before the stain goes on. Follow the finish maker's system from start to finish, because adhesion between its sealants and its stain is what the system was designed around.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="siding-trim" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Siding and Trim
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood siding is the most forgiving exterior wood to seal. It sheds water, gets angled rather than direct overhead sun, and does not take foot traffic. A good penetrating finish on siding commonly lasts several times longer than the same product on a deck. That makes siding the best place to choose a semi-transparent or toned oil over a pure clear, because the extra pigment pays off over a long service life.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at sun exposure side by side. South and west walls fade and dry out first; north walls grow mildew. A finish with a mildewcide is worth it on shaded walls, and you can recoat the sunny elevations more often without redoing the whole house. Penetrating oils blend well enough to allow that kind of spot maintenance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rough-sawn siding and shingles absorb far more finish than smooth boards and hold it longer. Plan on the low end of the label coverage figure, apply with a sprayer and back-brush or back-roll to work it into the texture. Smooth, planed siding takes less finish and may need a light sanding if it has mill glaze, the hard, shiny surface left by planer knives.
          </p>

          <p>
            Trim, doors and window casings are the one place where a film finish often makes sense. They are small, easy to inspect, protected by overhangs and visible up close. A marine-grade spar varnish or an exterior trim paint gives a crisp look. Use flexible exterior caulk at joints first so water does not get behind the film; our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">exterior caulk guide</a> covers the products that move with wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            For whole-house siding in cedar or redwood, a toned penetrating oil such as Penofin or TWP is the common professional choice because it can be maintained one wall at a time for decades without stripping. For painted or previously solid-stained siding, stay with a solid stain or paint, since a penetrating product cannot get through the existing film.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fences-structures" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Fences, Pergolas and Sheds
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fences and garden structures are vertical, which helps, but they also have huge surface area, two faces and countless end cuts. The best sealer for a fence is one you can spray quickly without lap marks, because nobody wants to back-brush 200 feet of pickets by hand. Oil-based penetrating products that tolerate spraying without heavy back-brushing are the usual pick; our <a href="/best-fence-stain/">fence stain guide</a> ranks those specifically.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pergolas and arbors are horizontal on top, so their beams and rafters weather like a deck. Seal the top faces and the end grain of every rafter tail more heavily than the posts. Rafter tails are often the first part of a pergola to check and rot because they collect water and catch direct sun all day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sheds and playsets can usually get by with a basic water repellent if you are comfortable recoating every year or two. For playsets, read the label for any guidance about surfaces children touch, and choose a product that cures fully without leaving a tacky film. Allow the full cure time before kids use the set.
          </p>

          <p>
            Posts in contact with the ground need more than a surface sealer. Ground-contact posts should be pressure-treated lumber rated for ground contact, and their field-cut ends should get an end-cut preservative. A sealer on the visible part of a post does nothing for the buried part, which is where fence posts actually fail.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your fence or structure is new pressure-treated lumber, it will not take a finish until it dries. Our guide to whether <a href="/can-you-stain-pressure-treated-wood/">you can stain pressure-treated wood</a> walks through the absorption test and typical drying timelines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="furniture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Outdoor Furniture and Teak
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outdoor furniture is usually made from dense, oily species such as teak, shorea, acacia or eucalyptus. These woods resist rot naturally, and left alone they weather to a silver gray that many owners like. Sealing them is about keeping the warm brown color and slowing surface checking, not about preventing decay.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dense woods need thin, penetrating finishes made for them. Teak sealers and teak oils are formulated to soak into tight grain; heavy deck sealers often sit on the surface and turn sticky. Apply thin coats with a rag or foam brush, wait the label's absorption time and wipe off every bit of excess. Leftover sealer on furniture becomes a gummy film that collects dirt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the furniture first. A teak cleaner or mild detergent with a soft brush removes gray fibers and mildew; a two-part teak cleaner brightens heavily weathered pieces. Let the wood dry fully, sand lightly with a fine grit to smooth raised grain, and then seal. Furniture is small enough that this takes an afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Teak oil and teak sealer are not the same thing. Traditional teak oils are often thinned drying oils that enrich the color but last only weeks to a few months outdoors. Teak sealers are formulated to last longer, often a season or more, and some include UV inhibitors. If you want low maintenance, choose a sealer over an oil.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store or cover furniture through winter if you can. Covered furniture dries out between rains less well, so use breathable covers and lift pieces off wet ground. A single fresh coat of sealer each spring keeps most hardwood furniture looking consistent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="logs-timbers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Log Walls, Timbers and Rough-Sawn Beams
          </h2>

          <p>
            Log homes and heavy timbers move more than any other exterior wood. They shrink and settle for years after construction, develop deep checks and expose large areas of end grain. A finish for logs has to flex with that movement, let trapped moisture escape and resist mildew on wide, rough surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most log-home finish systems are breathable semi-transparent stains made for that purpose, often paired with a compatible clear topcoat and with chinking or sealant in the joints. The products are designed as a system. Mixing a deck sealer into a log-home system usually ends with adhesion problems at the joints and uneven color.
          </p>

          <p>
            Upward-facing checks are the main entry point for water on logs and beams. The industry approach is to seal upward-facing checks wider than about a quarter inch with a flexible log sealant so water cannot sit in them. Downward-facing checks shed water on their own and are usually left open.
          </p>

          <p>
            Log ends that extend past the roofline are the most exposed wood on the building. They need extra finish, an end-grain treatment, and often a drip edge or cap. Expect to recoat south and west walls and log ends far more often than shaded, sheltered walls.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rough-sawn beams on porches and pergolas behave like small logs. They drink finish, so coverage per gallon drops sharply. Spray on a penetrating stain, back-brush into the texture and plan on more product than the label's smooth-wood figure suggests.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="docks-wet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Docks, Boardwalks and Wet Zones
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood that sits over water, beside a pool or in a wet garden spends far more time damp than ordinary decking. Mildew and algae grow faster, and finishes that depend on drying fully between rains struggle. Penetrating finishes are the only realistic choice here, because a film over constantly damp wood will lift.
          </p>

          <p>
            Overspray and runoff go straight into the water, so the choice of product matters for more than the wood. Many waterfront owners choose water-based penetrating sealers with lower solvent content, apply them by roller rather than sprayer and avoid working when rain is forecast. Some lakes and municipalities restrict what can be applied to docks; check local rules before you start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the boards clean. Algae on dock boards is a slip hazard and holds moisture. A deck cleaner and soft brush once or twice a season does more for both safety and longevity than any sealer. Avoid chlorine bleach near water; oxygen-based cleaners are gentler on wood and on aquatic life when rinsed thoroughly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pool decks add chlorine and splashing. Choose a product that stays slip resistant when wet, and recoat more often. If the pool surround is concrete rather than wood, you need a concrete product instead; see our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">best concrete sealers</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NEAR WATER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Apply by roller, not sprayer, over a lake or pond.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Spray drift and drips from any sealer end up in the water. Roll or brush instead, lay a tarp under the work area where you can, and do not apply when rain is due within the label's rain-free window.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="exposure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Long Sealers Last by Exposure
          </h2>

          <p>
            Life expectancy on a label usually assumes vertical surfaces. Horizontal wood that faces the sky and holds standing water wears much faster. A finish that lasts four or five years on north-facing siding might last only one or two on a south-facing deck. Plan maintenance by exposure, not by product name.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical recoat ranges</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1-2 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Clear finishes on horizontal wood</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-4 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Toned oils on fences and rails</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3-6 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Semi-transparent on shaded siding</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1 season</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Many teak oils on furniture</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Climate shifts those ranges. High-altitude and desert sun breaks finishes down faster. Humid, shaded climates grow mildew sooner. Freeze-thaw cycles open checks that let water in. A product that holds up for years in a mild coastal climate may need yearly maintenance at altitude in Colorado or in the Southwest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pigment buys time. The same base formula in a clear version will almost always need recoating sooner than its toned versions. If you want natural-looking wood with longer intervals, choose the lightest tinted option rather than a true clear. The color shift is usually subtle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the water drop test to decide. Sprinkle water on the surface; if it beads, the finish is working, and if it soaks in within a minute, it is time. Check sunny and shaded areas separately, and recoat only what needs it with penetrating products.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Applying Sealer to Vertical Wood
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean first with an oxygen-based wood cleaner and rinse at low pressure, keeping the nozzle well back from the surface. Follow with an oxalic acid brightener if the wood has darkened. Let the wood dry for the label's required time, often two days in good weather.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work from the top down so drips land on unfinished wood. On siding, coat a few courses at a time along their full length, ending at a natural break such as a corner or window. Stopping in the middle of a wall leaves a visible lap line, especially with water-based products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spraying speeds large jobs. A pump sprayer handles most thin penetrating finishes; an airless sprayer suits whole-house work. Follow every pass with a brush or roller to work finish into the grain and catch runs. On rough wood, back-brushing is the difference between a finish that lasts and one that sits in the texture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect what you do not want coated. Cover windows, plants, masonry and concrete; oil-based finishes stain stone and concrete. Wipe up drips immediately. Mask hardware and light fixtures, and keep a rag with mineral spirits or water handy, depending on the base.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the weather. Most labels want temperatures in a moderate range, no direct midday sun and no rain for a day or more. Water-based products are particularly sensitive to dew and cold nights before they set.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Maintenance and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Inspect exterior wood each spring. Look for gray, dry patches on sunny walls, mildew on shaded ones, and checks or splits on end grain. A simple cleaning and a fresh coat on worn areas keeps a penetrating finish going indefinitely. Waiting until the wood is badly weathered means heavier prep later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the same product family when you recoat. Switching from an oil to a water-based product, or from a penetrating finish to a film, can cause adhesion problems unless the old finish is fully weathered or removed. The label usually explains what it can go over.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store leftover finish tightly sealed, upright and above freezing for water-based products. Note the product, color and date so you can match it later. Partly full cans of oil finish can skin over; pour leftovers into a smaller container to reduce air space.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handle oily rags with care. Rags and pads soaked with linseed, tung and similar drying oils can heat and ignite as they cure. Spread them flat outdoors to dry, or put them in a sealed metal container filled with water until disposal.
          </p>

          <p>
            For decks specifically, start with our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain rankings</a>; for redwood decks and siding, our <a href="/best-redwood-sealer/">redwood sealer guide</a> covers products that keep the red tone longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a deck sealer on teak furniture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many deck sealers are formulated for softwoods and do not soak into dense teak, shorea or acacia. They sit on top, turn sticky and collect dirt. Use a finish made for dense hardwood, apply thin coats with a rag, wipe off every bit of excess and let it cure fully before using the furniture.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting film finishes on wood that moves a lot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Logs, rough timbers and wide boards swell and shrink with every season. A hard film such as paint or varnish cracks along that movement and lets water in behind it. Penetrating or breathable finishes made for those surfaces flex with the wood and can be refreshed without stripping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying near water without containment</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Overspray and runoff from dock and shoreline projects land directly in the water. Roll or brush instead of spraying, lay a tarp under the work area where possible and avoid working when rain is forecast. Check local rules, since some lakes and towns limit what can be applied to docks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring end grain and checks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                End grain absorbs water many times faster than the face of a board, and upward-facing checks hold water. Give ends an extra coat, use an end-cut preservative on field-cut treated lumber and seal wide upward-facing checks on logs and beams with a flexible log sealant.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing clear when you want color to last</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A clear finish keeps water out but lets sun break down the wood surface, so cedar, redwood and teak still drift toward gray. If keeping the warm tone matters, choose the lightest tinted version of a product. The pigment adds UV protection with only a small change in color.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

