        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which sealer suits most outdoor wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no single best sealer for every outdoor surface. For most siding, fences and decks, a toned penetrating oil such as Penofin Blue Label or TWP 1500 offers the best balance of protection and easy upkeep. Dense hardwood furniture needs a teak or hardwood sealer, log homes need a log-specific system, and wood near water benefits from a water-based penetrating product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many years does an exterior sealer protect wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On vertical siding and fences, a quality toned penetrating finish often lasts three to five years or more, depending on sun and climate. On horizontal decking, the same product may last only one to three years. Clear finishes and teak oils wear faster. Use the water drop test on sunny and shaded areas to decide when to recoat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal or stain exterior wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you want the natural color and accept frequent upkeep, a clear sealer works. If you want longer protection from sun and fewer recoats, a semi-transparent stain is the better choice because pigment blocks UV. Many products sit between the two as toned sealers. Solid stain or paint makes sense mainly on weathered, mismatched or previously coated wood.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use the same sealer on furniture and my deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes, but it is rarely ideal. Most deck sealers are formulated for softwoods and foot traffic, while outdoor furniture is usually dense hardwood that needs a thin finish made for tight grain. A versatile penetrating oil can work on both if the label lists hardwoods. Otherwise, buy a small container of teak sealer for the furniture.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does exterior wood need sealing every year?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. Furniture finished with teak oil may need it every season, and clear finishes on decks often need yearly attention. Toned penetrating oils on fences and siding can go several years between coats. The water drop test is the best guide: recoat when water soaks in quickly rather than on a fixed calendar.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is water-based or oil-based better for exterior wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Oil-based penetrating finishes typically soak deeper into dense or weathered wood and fade gradually. Water-based finishes dry faster, have less odor, clean up with soap and water and meet strict VOC rules. For docks and areas near water or plants, many owners prefer water-based products. Check the label for your species and climate before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I seal log ends with?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Log ends are end grain and absorb far more water than log faces, so they need extra attention. Apply additional coats of your log-home finish, and consider a product designed for end grain. Covering exposed ends with caps or flashing helps further. Ends that extend past the roofline weather fastest and should be inspected every year.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

